          "question": "My mother has heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, is on four heart-failure medicines, and her ankles are swelling again. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about those drug classes?",
          "answer": "The diagnostic specialist advises that if symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, medical advice should be sought. They note that while no emergency pattern in their red-flag table directly matched this description, this table covers a fixed list and other symptoms can still be serious. The research specialist states that worsening ankle swelling in someone with heart failure, especially while on several medications, could indicate worsening heart failure and fluid overload, which warrants prompt medical attention. They advise having your mother see a doctor or go to an urgent care center to assess her condition. Weight gain can be an early sign of fluid retention and often prompts contact with a clinician before breathlessness or swelling develops (lifestyle-heart-failure-diet). Many people with heart failure have an action plan that specifies thresholds for symptoms and what to do at each level; if one exists, it is the authority (lifestyle-heart-failure-diet).\n\nClinical guidance describes four foundational classes of medications for managing heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF) (condition-heart-failure, guideline-heart-failure-reduced-ef-therapy). These are typically started together or in quick succession rather than one at a time (condition-heart-failure, guideline-heart-failure-reduced-ef-therapy). Modern therapy with these medications has significantly improved outcomes, including reducing symptoms, hospital admissions, and mortality (condition-heart-failure, guideline-heart-failure-reduced-ef-therapy).\n\nThe four foundational drug classes for heart failure with reduced ejection fraction are:\n\n*   **ACE inhibitors, ARBs, or ARNIs:** These medications help to relax blood vessels, lower blood pressure, and reduce the workload on the heart (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment).\n*   **Beta-blockers:** These drugs slow the heart rate and reduce the force of the heart's contractions, which can improve heart function over time (guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention).\n*   **Mineralocorticoid Receptor Antagonists (MRAs):** These medications help the body get rid of excess fluid and sodium, and they also have protective effects on the heart (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment).\n*   **SGLT2 inhibitors:** This class of medication has recently been added to the foundational therapies for HFrEF and has shown significant benefits (guideline-heart-failure-reduced-ef-therapy, guideline-type-2-diabetes-first-line-therapy).\n\nThe diagnostic specialist notes that applying these therapies wholesale to heart failure with preserved ejection fraction can be an error, as some classes have not shown the same benefit in that group, with SGLT-2 inhibitors being a newer addition that has shown benefit (guideline-heart-failure-reduced-ef-therapy).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
